-- title:
--   Rescaling a minimal invariant set by a homothety
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $K\subseteq X$, $T:K\to K$ and $c\ne0$.
--
--   1. **Scaled bijection.** $K\to cK$, $x\mapsto cx$, with inverse $y\mapsto c^{-1}y$.
--   2. **Conjugated map.** $T_c:cK\to cK$,
--   $$
--   T_c(y)=c\,T(c^{-1}y).
--   $$
--   3. **Scaled data.** For $c>0$, minimal invariant data are carried to minimal invariant data on $cK$. Closedness, boundedness, convexity, separability, weak compactness, nonexpansiveness, absence of fixed points and minimality all survive. With $c=1/\operatorname{diam}K$ this normalizes the diameter to $1$.
